6. List (list)

A list stores multiple values.

Lists are ordered and mutable.

numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40]

print(numbers)

Output

[10, 20, 30, 40]

Mixed data types:

data = [1, "Suman", True, 4.5]
7. Tuple (tuple)

A tuple is like a list, but immutable.

student = ("Suman", 21, "Bangalore")

print(student)
Difference

List

numbers = [1, 2, 3]

Tuple

numbers = (1, 2, 3)
8. Set (set)

A set stores unique values.

nums = {1, 2, 3, 4}

print(nums)

Duplicate values are removed automatically.

nums = {1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3}

print(nums)

Output

{1, 2, 3}
9. Dictionary (dict)

Stores data as key-value pairs.

student = {
    "name": "Suman",
    "age": 21,
    "city": "Bangalore"
}

print(student)

Output

{'name': 'Suman', 'age': 21, 'city': 'Bangalore'}

Access values

print(student["name"])

Output

Suman
